In this lesson we'll look at how to pull the key features of a polynomial straight from its equation, before you even touch a pen to graph paper. First, glance at the degree and the leading coefficient. The degree tells you the shape, so a two gives you a parabola, a three gives you a cubic, and so on. The sign of the leading coefficient then tells you where the ends point, whether both arms lift up, both drop down, or one goes up and the other goes down. Next, find the y-intercept by setting x to zero. For any polynomial, that's just the constant term at the end, and it tells you exactly where the curve crosses the vertical axis. Then hunt for the x-intercepts. These are the values of x that make the whole expression equal to zero, and the factor theorem is your shortcut. Try simple candidates like positive one, negative one, or two. If it works, you have a factor and a root at the same time. Finally, look for any sign changes to spot where turning points might sit. Now let's see it in action.